const o=[{id:"C01",title:"WHO · Stress: questions and answers",url:"https://www.who.int/news-room/questions-and-answers/item/stress",type:"guidance",finding:"Describes stress as worry or mental tension in difficult situations. Suggests routine, rest, suitable activity, trusted connections, and managing news exposure.",limitation:"Public guidance, not a measured ODA effect. Suggestions need individual fit; they do not remove every stressor or replace care."},{id:"C02",title:"NHS · Get help with stress",url:"https://www.nhs.uk/mental-health/feelings-symptoms-behaviours/feelings-and-symptoms/stress/",type:"guidance",finding:"Stress can accompany physical, cognitive, emotional, and behavioral signs. Seek professional help when coping is difficult or self-help is not working.",limitation:"Signs such as tension, headaches, or poor concentration do not establish their cause. New or concerning symptoms may need assessment; naming is not diagnosis."},{id:"C03",title:"NHS Every Mind Matters · Anxiety",url:"https://www.nhs.uk/every-mind-matters/mental-health-issues/anxiety/",type:"guidance",finding:"Suggests gradual facing of safe feared situations, recording triggers, and care when anxiety affects daily life. Breathlessness may have another cause.",limitation:"Public guidance does not establish every feared situation as safe or validate ODA as exposure treatment. Leaving abuse or genuine danger is protective."},{id:"C04",title:"NHS · Panic disorder",url:"https://www.nhs.uk/mental-health/conditions/panic-disorder/",type:"guidance",finding:"Repeated panic attacks need professional assessment. Similar physical symptoms can occur in other conditions; treatment can include CBT and prescribed medication.",limitation:"New chest pain, faintness, or breathlessness is not automatically harmless panic. Breathing alone is not panic-disorder treatment; medication decisions belong with a prescriber."},{id:"C05",title:"NHS · 2026 · Breathing exercises for stress",url:"https://www.nhs.uk/mental-health/self-help/guides-tools-and-activities/breathing-exercises-for-stress/",type:"guidance",finding:"Offers supported, comfortable, gentle breathing without force, with regular practice. Counting is optional and should not cause strain.",limitation:"Public guidance gives no isolated trial effect, optimal ratio, or guarantee. Gentle breathing differs from the tested double-inhale protocol; shorter ODA pauses are adaptations."},{id:"C06",title:"NHS · 10 stressbusters",url:"https://www.nhs.uk/mental-health/self-help/guides-tools-and-activities/tips-to-reduce-stress/",type:"guidance",finding:"Suitable activity and trusted connection are coping options. Reduced emotional intensity does not mean the stressful circumstance has disappeared.",limitation:"Guidance, not standalone numerical evidence. Helping others may not fit an overextended person; listing contacts without asking is not evidence of support received."},{id:"C07",title:"Singh et al. · 2023 · Physical activity umbrella review",url:"https://www.newswise.com/pdf_docs/167719499939215_Singh%20et%20al%202023.pdf",type:"research",finding:"97 reviews covered 1,039 unique component trials. Depression median SMD was −0.43 (IQR −0.66 to −0.27); anxiety −0.42 (−0.66 to −0.26). Distress SMD −0.60 (95% CI −0.78 to −0.42) came from one six-trial review.",limitation:"77 reviews were critically low quality. Review-reported participant counts overlap. Medians and IQRs are not pooled effects and confidence intervals. Intensity/duration subgroup associations do not prescribe a personal dose or predict one walk."},{id:"C08",title:"Fincham et al. · 2023 · Breathwork trial meta-analysis",url:"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-27247-y",type:"research",finding:"The primary stress analysis combined 12 randomized trials with 785 adults: Hedges’ g −0.35 (95% CI −0.55 to −0.14). Techniques and populations varied.",limitation:"Moderate bias risk and limited follow-up constrain confidence. Subgroup results do not establish slow-breathing superiority, an optimal protocol, treatment equivalence, or equal safety across rapid and slow techniques."},{id:"C09",title:"Webb, Miles & Sheeran · 2012 · Emotion regulation meta-analysis",url:"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22582737/",type:"research",finding:"306 experimental comparisons found perspective change d+ 0.45, stimulus reappraisal 0.36, and thought suppression −0.12 on average.",limitation:"Abstract access; mainly induced short-term laboratory emotions. Thought and expression suppression differ. This does not show suppression always fails, a comforting interpretation is true, or lasting benefit from ODA prompts."},{id:"C10",title:"NHS Every Mind Matters · Reframing unhelpful thoughts",url:"https://www.nhs.uk/every-mind-matters/mental-wellbeing-tips/self-help-cbt-techniques/reframing-unhelpful-thoughts/",type:"guidance",finding:"Offers a structure of noticing a thought, checking evidence, and considering a balanced alternative. Real difficulties can need problem solving, acceptance, or support.",limitation:"Guidance, not a trial of ODA’s abbreviated worksheet. Balance can include uncertainty; positive interpretations need evidence and should not minimize mistreatment."},{id:"C11",title:"Balban et al. · 2023 · Structured respiration trial",url:"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.xcrm.2022.100895",type:"research",finding:"108 adults were randomized to five daily minutes of breathing or mindfulness for 28 days. Combined breathing positive-affect change averaged 1.91 points versus 1.22; cyclic sighing favored mindfulness in the model. Anxiety improved without significant group differences.",limitation:"Small groups, medical/psychiatric exclusions, retrospective registration, and no verified between-group confidence interval. Not evidence for two breaths, a universal technique ranking, or a proven causal mechanism. Significant sleep/HRV improvement was not established; durable anxiety benefit from this ODA pause is untested."},{id:"C13",title:"Torre & Lieberman · 2018 · Affect labeling review",url:"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/1754073917742706",type:"research",finding:"Narrative review discusses how putting feelings into words relates to several emotion-response measures. Naming is a plausible experiment.",limitation:"Abstract and references accessed. No pooled N or efficacy estimate verified. One word does not guarantee subjective relief or solve a stressor; the ODA situation-plus-label exercise is an adaptation."},{id:"C14",title:"Kross et al. · 2014 · Distanced self-talk",url:"https://doi.org/10.1037/a0035173",type:"research",finding:"Seven studies involved 585 participants. In one structured social-interaction study, non-first-person reflection improved observer-rated performance without differentially reducing anxiety beforehand.",limitation:"Laboratory social stress, not anxiety-disorder treatment or proof of instant calm. Exact independent replication and preregistration were not verified. ODA’s workplace action additions were not tested."},{id:"C17",title:"Edwards & Loprinzi · 2018 · Brief walking and meditation trial",url:"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6064756/",type:"research",finding:"66 young adults were randomized to ten minutes of treadmill walking, guided meditation, or quiet sitting. Fatigue and composite-mood interactions involved all three groups; walking’s within-group composite change had p=.10.",limitation:"Small acute trial; no verified effect confidence intervals or exact independent replication. Results do not clearly establish walking’s overall mood improvement or benefit for any accessible movement alternative."},{id:"C12",title:"Zaccaro et al. · 2018 · Slow-breathing physiology review",url:"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/human-neuroscience/articles/10.3389/fnhum.2018.00353/full",type:"research",finding:"Qualitative synthesis of 15 healthy-participant studies associated slow breathing with physiological and psychological measures. There was no pooled effect estimate.",limitation:"Small heterogeneous within-person or pre/post studies, not randomized longitudinal evidence. Physiological associations do not prove a vagal mental-health mechanism, an optimal rate, or treatment of anxiety disorders."},{id:"cyclic-sighing",title:"Balban et al. · 2023 · Structured respiration trial",url:"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9873947/",type:"research",finding:"108 adults were randomized to five daily minutes of breathing or mindfulness for 28 days. Cyclic sighing improved daily positive affect and reduced breathing rate more than mindfulness. Anxiety changes did not significantly differ between groups.",limitation:"Small remote study, young mostly university-linked sample, medical/psychiatric exclusions and retrospective registration. No established benefit from two breaths, instant cortisol change, significant sleep/HRV improvement or durable anxiety treatment."},{id:"slow-breathing",title:"Zaccaro et al. · 2018 · Slow-breathing physiology review",url:"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/human-neuroscience/articles/10.3389/fnhum.2018.00353/full",type:"research",finding:"Qualitative synthesis of 15 healthy-participant studies associated slow breathing with physiological and psychological measures. There was no pooled effect estimate.",limitation:"Small heterogeneous within-person or pre/post studies, not randomized longitudinal evidence. Physiological associations do not prove a vagal mental-health mechanism, an optimal rate, or treatment of anxiety disorders."},{id:"activity-mood",title:"Singh et al. · 2023 · Physical activity umbrella review",url:"https://www.newswise.com/pdf_docs/167719499939215_Singh%20et%20al%202023.pdf",type:"research",finding:"97 reviews covered 1,039 unique component trials. Depression median SMD was −0.43 (IQR −0.66 to −0.27); anxiety −0.42 (−0.66 to −0.26). Distress SMD −0.60 (95% CI −0.78 to −0.42) came from one six-trial review.",limitation:"77 reviews were critically low quality. Review-reported participant counts overlap. Medians and IQRs are not pooled effects and confidence intervals. Intensity/duration subgroup associations do not prescribe a personal dose or predict one walk."},{id:"emotion-regulation",title:"Webb, Miles & Sheeran · 2012 · Emotion regulation meta-analysis",url:"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22582737/",type:"research",finding:"306 experimental comparisons found perspective change d+ 0.45, stimulus reappraisal 0.36, and thought suppression −0.12 on average.",limitation:"Abstract access; mainly induced short-term laboratory emotions. Thought and expression suppression differ. This does not show suppression always fails, a comforting interpretation is true, or lasting benefit from ODA prompts."},{id:"affect-labeling",title:"Torre & Lieberman · 2018 · Affect labeling review",url:"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/1754073917742706",type:"research",finding:"Narrative review discusses how putting feelings into words relates to several emotion-response measures. Naming is a plausible experiment.",limitation:"Abstract and references accessed. No pooled N or efficacy estimate verified. One word does not guarantee subjective relief or solve a stressor; the ODA situation-plus-label exercise is an adaptation."},{id:"who-stress",title:"WHO · Stress: questions and answers",url:"https://www.who.int/news-room/questions-and-answers/item/stress",type:"guidance",finding:"Describes stress as worry or mental tension in difficult situations. Suggests routine, rest, suitable activity, trusted connections, and managing news exposure.",limitation:"Public guidance, not a measured ODA effect. Suggestions need individual fit; they do not remove every stressor or replace care."},{id:"nhs-stress",title:"NHS · Get help with stress",url:"https://www.nhs.uk/mental-health/feelings-symptoms-behaviours/feelings-and-symptoms/stress/",type:"guidance",finding:"Stress can accompany physical, cognitive, emotional, and behavioral signs. Seek professional help when coping is difficult or self-help is not working.",limitation:"Signs such as tension, headaches, or poor concentration do not establish their cause. New or concerning symptoms may need assessment; naming is not diagnosis."},{id:"nhs-stress-tips",title:"NHS · 10 stressbusters",url:"https://www.nhs.uk/mental-health/self-help/guides-tools-and-activities/tips-to-reduce-stress/",type:"guidance",finding:"Suitable activity and trusted connection are coping options. Reduced emotional intensity does not mean the stressful circumstance has disappeared.",limitation:"Guidance, not standalone numerical evidence. Helping others may not fit an overextended person; listing contacts without asking is not evidence of support received."},{id:"calm-nhs-anxiety",title:"NHS Every Mind Matters · Anxiety",url:"https://www.nhs.uk/every-mind-matters/mental-health-issues/anxiety/",type:"guidance",finding:"Suggests gradual facing of safe feared situations, recording triggers, and care when anxiety affects daily life. Breathlessness may have another cause.",limitation:"Public guidance does not establish every feared situation as safe or validate ODA as exposure treatment. Leaving abuse or genuine danger is protective."},{id:"calm-sleep-regularity",title:"Windred et al. · 2024 · Sleep regularity and mortality",url:"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10782501/",type:"research",finding:"60,977 UK Biobank adults had seven-day actigraphy; 1,859 died during follow-up. The highest regularity quintile had roughly 30% lower all-cause and 38% lower cardiometabolic mortality than the lowest in fully adjusted models. Regularity predicted mortality better than duration.",limitation:"Observational, mostly white UK sample. Confounding remains possible. Read the primary full text through PMC; association does not establish prevention, a personal benefit, or a 30-minute treatment threshold."},{id:"calm-caffeine-meta",title:"Gardiner et al. · 2023 · Caffeine and subsequent sleep",url:"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36870101/",type:"research",finding:"Meta-analysis of 24 studies: caffeine reduced total sleep time by 45 minutes and increased sleep-onset latency by 9 minutes. Modelled cutoffs were 8.8 hours before bed for 107 mg coffee and 13.2 hours for 217.5 mg pre-workout caffeine.",limitation:"Primary abstract read through PubMed; publisher DOI blocked. Mainly controlled studies in young adults. Dose, metabolism and sensitivity vary; modelled cutoffs are starting points rather than guaranteed safe times."},{id:"calm-postmeal-walking",title:"Buffey et al. · 2022 · Walking breaks and post-meal metabolism",url:"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40279-022-01649-4",type:"research",finding:"Seven acute crossover trials compared walking breaks, standing and sitting. Walking lowered post-meal glucose (standardised difference −0.72) and insulin (−0.83) versus sitting, and outperformed standing.",limitation:"Small set of largely laboratory studies; low external validity and heterogeneous protocols. This is evidence for repeated sitting breaks, not guaranteed results from a 20-minute walk, weight loss, or cortisol reduction."},{id:"calm-magnesium-evidence",title:"NCCIH · Magnesium supplements for sleep disorders",url:"https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/in-the-news-magnesium-supplements-for-sleep-disorders",type:"guidance",finding:"NCCIH reports insufficient rigorous evidence for magnesium supplements in sleep disorders. Its summary of Mah and Pitre’s 2021 review describes three low-quality trials with 151 older adults. High supplemental intake can cause diarrhoea, nausea and cramps.",limitation:"NCCIH guidance opened; Mah and Pitre’s primary page was unavailable and is not separately cited. Findings do not establish glycinate superiority, routine supplementation, or a reliable two-week benefit."},{id:"calm-magnesium-food",title:"NIH ODS · Magnesium: food, assessment and safety",url:"https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Magnesium-HealthProfessional/",type:"guidance",finding:"Leafy greens, legumes, nuts, seeds and whole grains provide magnesium. Serum levels incompletely reflect body stores. The US adult upper limit is 350 mg/day from supplements and medicines, excluding food, unless a clinician advises otherwise.",limitation:"Nutritional and safety guidance, not evidence that adding magnesium lowers cortisol or treats insomnia. Assessment may need clinical context; supplement interactions and kidney function matter."},{id:"calm-ashwagandha",title:"Bachour et al. · 2025 · Ashwagandha conference abstract",url:"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12242034/",type:"research",finding:"Conference abstract: 15 randomized trials, 873 adults. At eight weeks, ashwagandha groups had lower cortisol, perceived stress and anxiety scores than placebo. Quality of life did not significantly improve.",limitation:"Reviewed for a conference, not through standard BJPsych Open peer review. Short studies, different extracts and limited reporting constrain confidence; statistical differences do not establish universal benefit or long-term safety."},{id:"calm-ashwagandha-safety",title:"Merck Manual Professional · 2025 · Ashwagandha",url:"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/special-subjects/dietary-supplements/ashwagandha",type:"guidance",finding:"Describes limited benefit evidence, liver-injury reports, sedation and interactions with thyroid hormones and sedatives. Advises against use in pregnancy and breastfeeding.",limitation:"Clinical reference, not a risk-incidence estimate. Extracts vary and long-term safety is uncertain. Thyroid or liver conditions and interacting medicines require professional advice."},{id:"calm-hpa-sleep",title:"van Dalfsen & Markus · 2018 · Sleep and HPA stress reactivity",url:"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29126903/",type:"research",finding:"Abstract: poorer objective or subjective sleep quality was linked with increased HPA cortisol reactivity to laboratory stress. Normal variations in sleep duration did not appear to affect reactivity; excessive daytime sleepiness was linked with a blunted response.",limitation:"Abstract only. Findings concern responses to laboratory stress, not proof of a flat daily cortisol curve, next-day effects from every short night, or efficacy of this habit package."},{id:"calm-who-activity",title:"WHO · 2020 · Physical activity and sedentary behaviour guidelines",url:"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7719906/",type:"guidance",finding:"WHO’s 2020 adult guidelines recommend 150 to 300 minutes of moderate activity weekly, or the vigorous equivalent, and muscle strengthening on two or more days. Begin with manageable amounts and increase gradually.",limitation:"Public health guidance, not a cortisol protocol. No universal three-to-four hard-session rule, fixed rest-day count, or lighter week every six to eight weeks; rest and progression need individual fit."},{id:"calm-cortisol-rhythm",title:"MedlinePlus · Cortisol test and daily variation",url:"https://medlineplus.gov/lab-tests/cortisol-test/",type:"guidance",finding:"Cortisol helps the body respond to stress and normally varies across the day, with higher morning and lower later-day levels. Clinical interpretation may require several tests.",limitation:"Patient guidance about medical testing, not a symptom checklist for self-diagnosis. Too little cortisol can also be harmful; lower is not always better, and medicines should not be stopped independently."}],a=[{schemaVersion:2,revision:2,id:"calm-1",courseId:"calm",locale:"en",title:"Catch the reply you might regret",goal:"Name one familiar stress moment and make one safe useful response before sending or reacting.",minutes:{learning:6,suggestedAction:2,review:2},direction:{hook:"Your jaw is tight and the reply is ready. Choose the next move before you press send.",reason:{id:"calm-1-reason",label:"What does your usual stress response cost you?",kind:"text",maxLength:160,optional:!0,suggestions:["Send replies I regret","Leave priorities unclear","Carry work tension into home","No previous occasion"]},why:{id:"calm-1-why",label:"What would this move protect in your week?",kind:"text",maxLength:300,optional:!0}},idea:{paragraphs:["Tension, headaches, and poor concentration can accompany stress, but they do not identify the cause. Treat a familiar signal as a cue to choose a response, rather than a diagnosis. New or worrying physical symptoms need assessment.","Name a feeling and the situation: “Worried about this deadline.” Naming is a reasonable experiment, not a guarantee of relief. Then do something useful: postpone an impulsive reply, ask which task takes priority, set a safe boundary, or seek support. Keep this cue and response as your experiment through the course. Feeling calmer is not the required result."],example:{title:"Priya changes the reply",text:"Priya sees another deadline change at her desk. Her familiar jaw tension appears and she wants to send a sharp reply. She names “worried about the workload,” then asks which task should take priority. Her manager has not replied by lunch. Priya records her question and the silence. She is still worried."}},move:{invitation:"Keep your response; edit the deadline example.",instructions:["Name the moment without diagnosing its cause.","Use one safe question, pause, boundary, or support request."],prompts:[],suggestions:{action:["Ask which of two deadlines comes first","Delay one impulsive reply","Send workload facts without a sharp comment"],minimum:["Send one question naming both deadlines","Keep one reply unsent for a minute","Send one factual sentence about the workload"],cue:["When a deadline-change email arrives","When I notice my familiar signal","Before I send a sharp reply"],place:["At my desk, in the email thread","A private safe pause space","My accessible communication space"],minutes:[2,1,5],fallback:["Leave draft unsent; send only the priority question","Choose a protective boundary","Ask an available person for support"],observation:["Question sent; reply or no reply","Whether the impulse became a send","Which signal and response occurred"],when:[{label:"Today",daysFromToday:0},{label:"Tomorrow",daysFromToday:1}]},suggested:{action:"Ask which of two deadlines comes first",minimum:"Send one question naming both deadlines",minutes:2,cueHint:"When a deadline-change email arrives",placeHint:"At my desk, in the email thread",obstacleHint:"I start composing a sharp reply",fallbackHint:"Leave draft unsent; send only the priority question",observationHint:"Question sent; reply or no reply"},stopRule:"New or worrying symptoms need assessment. Use protection or support if the situation is unsafe.",accessibleAlternative:"Use outward cues, typing, or supported communication. For a support request, choose Adapt and Support; name its own minimum."},result:{question:"Did you use the response you chose?",observationPrompt:"What did you do after the signal, and what actually followed?"},judgment:{primary:{id:"calm-1-decision",scenario:"Priya receives a deadline change. She is safe, but wants to send a sharp reply before clarifying priorities.",question:"Which response uses the signal well?",options:[{id:"calm-1-full-explanation",text:"Send a detailed explanation of the workload immediately, then take a break before reading the reply.",feedback:"Explaining the workload can be useful, but an impulsive detailed message may miss the priority decision Priya needs. She can first ask one clear question without claiming the deadline is settled."},{id:"calm-1-name-only",text:"Name the worry privately and leave the workload unchanged until she feels ready to discuss it.",feedback:"Naming describes the moment but does not decide priorities or make a request. Priya can remain worried while using one safe question that addresses the actual work problem."},{id:"calm-1-priority-question",text:"Name the deadline worry, hold the sharp reply, and ask which task should take priority.",feedback:"This connects noticing to a useful act. The manager may not answer promptly, so Priya records what she asked and chooses a fallback without treating silence as agreement."}],bestOptionId:"calm-1-priority-question"}},carry:{takeaway:"Name the moment, then choose a response you can actually observe.",followUp:{questionId:"calm-1-return",question:"Did the familiar signal lead to your chosen response?",observationPrompt:"What happened when your chosen cue arrived?",adjustmentPrompt:"What should you repeat, change, or get help with?"}},goDeeper:{sections:[{heading:"Signals are useful without becoming diagnoses",paragraphs:["NHS guidance lists physical, cognitive, emotional, and behavioral signs that can accompany stress [C02]. A familiar tight jaw, irritability, or scattered attention can be useful information to notice. Those signs still do not establish stress as their cause. They can have other explanations, and new or concerning symptoms should not be brushed aside as ordinary tension. Naming a familiar moment is a cue for a choice, not a diagnosis.","This lesson asks you to choose one recognizable cue, not search your body until you find a problem. You can use an outward cue, such as opening a difficult email, with your eyes open and your usual communication method. The cue creates an opportunity to choose a safe question, pause, boundary, or support request. That is an ODA planning adaptation. It does not show that everyone has a stable symptom pattern or that noticing alone changes health."]},{heading:"A label is not the whole response",paragraphs:["Torre and Lieberman’s narrative review discusses affect labeling, putting feelings into words, across several emotion-response measures [C13]. The accessed abstract and references supplied no pooled participant count or numerical efficacy estimate. Subjective relief is not guaranteed. A one-word label followed by a situation is an ODA adaptation, rather than a precisely reproduced treatment or brain-reset procedure.","NHS thought-checking guidance supports separating a reaction from an evidence-based next choice [C10]. “I am worried about the workload” can coexist with a real workload problem. You might need clearer priorities, practical help, or protection rather than a nicer story. The continuing experiment records what followed the cue, including a question without a reply. Later lessons offer a tool only when an observed barrier makes it relevant. You can choose support immediately, without waiting to master all the tools."]}],sourceIds:["C02","C13","C10"]}},{schemaVersion:2,revision:2,id:"calm-2",courseId:"calm",locale:"en",title:"You do not have to perform calm",goal:"Try a comfortable seated pause, then return to your chosen safe response.",minutes:{learning:6,suggestedAction:2,review:2},direction:{hook:"Waiting to feel calm can delay the conversation again. Choose a comfortable pause that leaves you free to act.",reason:{id:"calm-2-reason",label:"What useful response are you waiting to feel ready for?",kind:"text",maxLength:160,optional:!0,suggestions:["Asking which deadline comes first","Saying I cannot cover another shift","Requesting help arranging care","No response I am delaying"]},why:{id:"calm-2-why",label:"What would this move protect in your week?",kind:"text",maxLength:300,optional:!0}},idea:{paragraphs:["A study of 108 adults tested five minutes daily for 28 days, not two breaths. Anxiety improved in the breathing and mindfulness groups without a significant difference between them. That does not identify one quick breathing method as the answer for everyone.","If a pause might help your saved response, sit safely with eyes open. Gentle unforced breathing is optional. You can instead notice an ordinary object while breathing normally. Stop if unwell. Then choose your safe next move; calm is not an entry requirement. A shorter pause does not inherit the study’s effect."],example:{title:"Priya stops the exercise",text:"Priya tries gentle breathing in a chair at home before asking about tomorrow’s workload. She feels light-headed, stops immediately, and breathes normally while staying seated. Once well, she sends the priority question. She records the stop and the question separately. The pause was not comfortable enough to repeat, so she chooses an outward anchor next time."}},move:{invitation:"Keep your response; edit the deadline example.",instructions:["Sit safely; try ordinary breathing with an outward anchor or gentle unforced breathing.","Stop if unwell. When suitable, use your chosen safe response."],prompts:[],suggestions:{action:["Pause; ask which deadline comes first","Notice object; ask which deadline comes first","Send the priority question without a pause"],minimum:["Send one question naming both deadlines","Send one clear boundary sentence","Send the deadline priority question without breathing"],cue:["Before replying to a deadline change","When a deadline-change email arrives","Before replying about an extra shift"],place:["Supported chair at home, with my phone","At my desk, in the email thread","A seated space with eyes open"],minutes:[2,1,5],fallback:["End pause; send the deadline priority question","Skip breathing; request help with the workload","Stop; stay seated until symptoms pass"],observation:["Pause comfort; question and reply or silence","Whether I used the chosen response","Any discomfort and actual action"],when:[{label:"Today",daysFromToday:0},{label:"Tomorrow",daysFromToday:1}]},suggested:{action:"Pause; ask which deadline comes first",minimum:"Send one question naming both deadlines",minutes:2,cueHint:"Before replying to a deadline change",placeHint:"Supported chair at home, with my phone",obstacleHint:"I keep checking for calm after a comfortable pause",fallbackHint:"End pause; send the deadline priority question",observationHint:"Pause comfort; question and reply or silence"},stopRule:"Stop if dizzy or unwell; sit and breathe normally. New or concerning symptoms need assessment; severe chest pain or breathlessness needs urgent care.",accessibleAlternative:"Eyes open and outward anchor are valid. No breath holds, forced breathing, or driving while symptomatic."},result:{question:"Did you make your chosen response safely?",observationPrompt:"What pause or stop happened, and did your next response happen?"},judgment:{primary:{id:"calm-2-decision",scenario:"Priya feels light-headed during breathing at home. She wants to finish before sending her prepared question.",question:"What fits the stop rule?",options:[{id:"calm-2-restart-later",text:"Pause until the sensation passes, then restart the planned exercise before sending the question.",feedback:"Restarting treats the planned dose as an obligation despite discomfort. Priya should stop the exercise, use ordinary breathing safely, and assess symptoms rather than earn permission to ask."},{id:"calm-2-stop-normal",text:"Stop, breathe normally while safely seated, and seek assessment if symptoms persist or are concerning.",feedback:"Safety comes before completing an exercise. Priya can choose an outward anchor or skip practice later; new or concerning symptoms should not be assumed to be merely stress."},{id:"calm-2-gentler-finish",text:"Reduce the effort and finish with softer breaths, then decide whether to use a different method tomorrow.",feedback:"Softer breathing can sound like an adaptation, but continuing after light-headedness contradicts the stop rule. Stop now, remain safely seated, and use ordinary breathing instead of finishing."}],bestOptionId:"calm-2-stop-normal"}},carry:{takeaway:"A useful tool respects your limits and leaves the next response yours to choose.",followUp:{questionId:"calm-2-return",question:"Was the pause comfortable, and did it support or delay your chosen response?",observationPrompt:"What happened when your chosen cue arrived?",adjustmentPrompt:"What should you repeat, change, or get help with?"}},goDeeper:{sections:[{heading:"The trial does not establish a quick reset",paragraphs:["Balban and colleagues randomized 108 adults into small groups for five daily minutes over 28 days [C11]. Combined breathing groups had a mean daily positive-affect change of 1.91 points versus 1.22 for mindfulness. Cyclic sighing favored mindfulness in a statistical model. Those combined raw means do not identify a cyclic-sighing-only mean or prove a universal ranking. Anxiety improved without a significant between-group difference.","The exploratory trial had medical and psychiatric exclusions and was registered retrospectively. It did not establish significant improvements in sleep or heart-rate variability. Nor did it prove a vagal mechanism or a benefit from two breaths. These results do not establish durable anxiety benefit from this shorter pause. This lesson chooses a comfortable voluntary pause rather than prescribing the study’s double-inhale routine. That shorter adaptation has not been tested as equivalent to the original protocol."]},{heading:"Comfort is part of the choice",paragraphs:["NHS breathing guidance emphasizes supported comfort and gentle breaths without force [C05]. Counting is optional; a precise inhale-to-exhale ratio is not required. This public instruction is practical guidance, not an isolated randomized dose. Fuller, faster, or more effortful breathing is not an instruction to pursue here. Keeping eyes open, breathing normally, and noticing a familiar object are available alternatives.","NHS panic information cautions that similar physical sensations can occur in other conditions [C04]. Stop an exercise if dizzy or unwell and remain safely seated with normal breathing. Do not drive while symptomatic. Persistent or concerning symptoms need assessment; severe or urgent symptoms need local emergency care. Recurrent panic or impairment also deserves professional help. Your record can show a safe stop, an uncomfortable practice, and a later priority question. Those are distinct facts, not a failed demand to feel calm."]}],sourceIds:["C04","C05","C11"]}},{schemaVersion:2,revision:2,id:"calm-3",courseId:"calm",locale:"en",title:"A break does not need a workout",goal:"Use suitable brief movement only if it helps your continuing cue-and-response experiment fit your day.",minutes:{learning:6,suggestedAction:2,review:2},direction:{hook:"The full workout cannot happen today. Is a smaller break useful before the response you still need to make?",reason:{id:"calm-3-reason",label:"What did your last skipped break cost? No recent occasion is valid.",kind:"text",maxLength:160,optional:!0,suggestions:["Kept rushing through replies","Carried tension into the next task","No recent skipped break"]},why:{id:"calm-3-why",label:"What would this move protect in your week?",kind:"text",maxLength:300,optional:!0}},idea:{paragraphs:["Exercise programs can reduce distress on average, but their results do not predict one short walk. In a small ten-minute trial, walking’s overall mood change was not statistically clear. A useful break does not need to produce a better mood to count as an attempt.","If movement fits before your saved response, try a comfortable accessible version. Keep the cue, then return to that response. Seated movement or a nonmovement pause can fit your needs, without inheriting the walking study’s effect. Record the version and actual duration. Keep your original move if it already works."],example:{title:"Priya keeps the small version",text:"Priya planned a short walk before her afternoon priority check. Rain and a delivery leave her only two minutes. She uses comfortable seated movement instead, then sends her prepared question. Her tension feels unchanged. She records the version, duration, and sent question. Tomorrow she can repeat that workable arrangement without adding minutes as a debt."}},move:{invitation:"Keep your response; edit the seated-break example.",instructions:["Add movement only when it addresses a barrier in your saved experiment.","For a first plan, try one seated minute before sending the prepared priority question.","If movement is unsuitable, edit the action and minimum to the question alone."],prompts:[],suggestions:{action:["Move seated; ask which deadline comes first","Walk briefly; send the priority question","Send the priority question without movement"],minimum:["One seated minute; send the priority question","One walking minute; send the priority question","Send one question naming both deadlines"],cue:["After lunch, before the afternoon priority check","After my usual lunch break","When a deadline-change email arrives"],place:["Supported chair at my desk","My supported chair","A familiar safe walking route"],minutes:[2,5,10],fallback:["Skip movement; send the deadline priority question","Keep the response; skip unsuitable movement","Ask for suitable movement guidance"],observation:["Actual movement minutes; question and reply or silence","Any discomfort during the break","Unchanged feelings are valid observations"],when:[{label:"Today",daysFromToday:0},{label:"Tomorrow",daysFromToday:1}]},suggested:{action:"Move seated; ask which deadline comes first",minimum:"One seated minute; send the priority question",minutes:2,cueHint:"After lunch, before the afternoon priority check",placeHint:"Supported chair at my desk",obstacleHint:"Pain, access, or time makes movement unsuitable",fallbackHint:"Skip movement; send the deadline priority question",observationHint:"Actual movement minutes; question and reply or silence"},stopRule:"Stop movement for pain, dizziness, or feeling unwell; seek suitable advice. Do not use movement as punishment.",accessibleAlternative:"Use seated movement or a nonmovement pause. If skipping movement, save the question alone as action and minimum."},result:{question:"Did you use your chosen break and response?",observationPrompt:"What version and duration happened, and what did you do next?"},judgment:{primary:{id:"calm-3-decision",scenario:"Priya notices she rushes replies after skipping breaks. She has two minutes, a prepared priority question, and suitable seated movement; rain blocks her walk.",question:"Which plan best tests her observed rushing problem?",options:[{id:"calm-3-larger-tomorrow",text:"Send the question now and reserve a walk for a later break when the weather allows.",feedback:"Sending now handles priorities promptly, and a later walk remains possible. It leaves her observed rushing barrier untested; a suitable minute still fits before the prepared question."},{id:"calm-3-research-first",text:"Use the two minutes to compare movement routines, then choose a route before asking for priorities.",feedback:"Research can help when a suitable option is missing. Here Priya already has one. Another comparison replaces the actual break and question with preparation that does not resolve today’s constraint."},{id:"calm-3-suitable-small",text:"Use her suitable seated minute, send the prepared question, and record the version without requiring relief.",feedback:"This fits the available time and keeps the useful response. Seated movement is an adaptation, not the tested walking protocol, and unchanged tension is an honest observation."}],bestOptionId:"calm-3-suitable-small"}},carry:{takeaway:"Use the version that fits. Do not turn a missed break into tomorrow’s debt.",followUp:{questionId:"calm-3-return",question:"Did the break fit your body and leave time for the chosen response?",observationPrompt:"What happened when your chosen cue arrived?",adjustmentPrompt:"What should you repeat, change, or get help with?"}},goDeeper:{sections:[{heading:"Program averages are not one-break predictions",paragraphs:["Singh and colleagues synthesized 97 reviews covering 1,039 unique component randomized trials [C07]. Depression and anxiety results were median standardized effects across reviews, not one pooled participant-level estimate. Depression was −0.43 with an interquartile range of −0.66 to −0.27; anxiety was −0.42 with a range of −0.66 to −0.26. These ranges are not confidence intervals. The review-reported participant counts contain overlap.","The distress estimate of −0.60, with a 95% confidence interval from −0.78 to −0.42, came from one review of six trials. Seventy-seven reviews were rated critically low quality. Associations with intensity or program duration do not show that harder or shorter is best for a particular person. The broad evidence supports considering suitable activity alongside other support. It does not justify escalating a two-minute break or promising that today’s tension will fall."]},{heading:"What a brief walking experiment can tell you",paragraphs:["Edwards and Loprinzi randomized 66 young adults to ten minutes of treadmill walking, guided meditation, or quiet sitting [C17]. The fatigue and composite-mood interactions compared all three groups. They were not walking-only effect estimates. Walking’s within-group composite-mood change had p=.10, so the detailed results do not clearly establish an overall mood benefit from that walk. Effect confidence intervals and independent exact replication were not verified.","An indoor route, comfortable pace, seated movement, or nonmovement pause is an ODA adaptation, not that laboratory procedure. NHS guidance treats movement as one coping option while noting it does not remove the stressor [C06]. Use the record to assess suitability, access, and whether your planned next response happened. One before-and-after feeling change cannot prove what caused it. A health condition, pain, or eating concern may call for qualified advice rather than a bigger activity target."]}],sourceIds:["C06","C07","C17"]}},{schemaVersion:2,revision:2,id:"calm-4",courseId:"calm",locale:"en",title:"“Can we talk?” Check your story",goal:"Separate observation from prediction, then use one safe practical response.",minutes:{learning:6,suggestedAction:2,review:2},direction:{hook:"One vague message, a whole ending in your head. Check what you know before your next move.",reason:{id:"calm-4-reason",label:"What are you postponing because a prediction feels settled?",kind:"text",maxLength:160,optional:!0,suggestions:["Asking what to prepare","Sending a correction I can already make","Requesting help with an unsafe situation","No action I am postponing"]},why:{id:"calm-4-why",label:"What would this move protect in your week?",kind:"text",maxLength:300,optional:!0}},idea:{paragraphs:["A balanced thought is not necessarily a positive thought. NHS guidance asks you to check evidence and consider a fuller view. “I do not know yet” can be more accurate than either “I am in trouble” or “They must value me.” Neither imagined motive is a fact.","Return to the same cue and response. If a prediction blocked it, name what was said or done, then choose a safe clarification, repair, boundary, or support step. Reframing does not remove a real workload problem or mistreatment. Test the action and evidence that follow, even if uncertainty remains."],example:{title:"Priya asks what to prepare",text:"Priya receives “Can we talk tomorrow?” and predicts she caused a problem. She records the actual message, then asks what to prepare for their safe check-in. Her manager replies, “The client’s revised schedule.” That gives her a useful task, not certainty about the whole meeting. She prepares the schedule and records the exchange while still nervous."}},move:{invitation:"Keep your response; edit the preparation-question example.",instructions:["Separate the observation from your prediction.","Ask, repair, protect, or seek support; do not invent another person’s motive."],prompts:[],suggestions:{action:["Ask what to prepare for the meeting","Clarify one ambiguous request","Make one safe repair or boundary"],minimum:["Send one preparation question","Ask one clarification question","Take one chosen protective step"],cue:["When a meeting message omits the topic","When a difficult work email arrives","Before I act on a prediction"],place:["At my desk, in the supervisor’s email","A private supported communication space","My accessible note-and-contact space"],minutes:[2,1,5],fallback:["Bring the current schedule; ask at the meeting","Choose protection or support","Wait for a safer clarification opening"],observation:["Question sent; topic learned or still unknown","Facts learned, including continued uncertainty","What safe step I actually took"],when:[{label:"Today",daysFromToday:0},{label:"Tomorrow",daysFromToday:1}]},suggested:{action:"Ask what to prepare for the meeting",minimum:"Send one preparation question",minutes:2,cueHint:"When a meeting message omits the topic",placeHint:"At my desk, in the supervisor’s email",obstacleHint:"The safe supervisor cannot answer before the meeting",fallbackHint:"Bring the current schedule; ask at the meeting",observationHint:"Question sent; topic learned or still unknown"},stopRule:"Do not reframe mistreatment into acceptance. Use safety, boundaries, or support when needed.",accessibleAlternative:"Use writing, assistive communication, or a trusted supporter; no body focus is needed."},result:{question:"Did you take the fact-based step you chose?",observationPrompt:"What did you ask or do, and what evidence actually followed?"},judgment:{primary:{id:"calm-4-decision",scenario:"Priya gets “Can we talk tomorrow?” from a safe supervisor who can answer a short question today. She has ten minutes and the current deliverable, but no topic.",question:"Which response checks the prediction?",options:[{id:"calm-4-positive-story",text:"List the reasons her supervisor might appreciate her work, then prepare for a positive conversation tomorrow.",feedback:"A positive explanation can be possible, but it is still a guessed motive. Priya can leave the outcome uncertain and ask what to prepare instead of settling a reassuring story."},{id:"calm-4-clarify",text:"Note the exact message, ask what to prepare, and use the reply to choose a practical step.",feedback:"This separates known facts from prediction and gathers relevant information. The reply may leave uncertainty; Priya still gains a clearer action without needing to feel reassured or assume a motive."},{id:"calm-4-review-everything",text:"Check the current deliverable for ten minutes and bring it tomorrow, without asking about the topic.",feedback:"A bounded check may improve the deliverable. It may prepare the wrong material here, because the topic is unknown and the supervisor can clarify now. Worry is not evidence of an error."}],bestOptionId:"calm-4-clarify"}},carry:{takeaway:"A fuller view can say “I do not know yet” and still choose a useful action.",followUp:{questionId:"calm-4-return",question:"Did the action add evidence, or did you replace one guess with another?",observationPrompt:"What happened when your chosen cue arrived?",adjustmentPrompt:"What should you repeat, change, or get help with?"}},goDeeper:{sections:[{heading:"Balance does not require a nicer certainty",paragraphs:["NHS guidance on reframing uses noticing, checking evidence, and considering a balanced alternative [C10]. A real observation might be “The message asks to talk tomorrow.” A prediction might be “I will lose my role.” A possible alternative is “There may be a schedule issue.” The alternative remains a possibility until evidence arrives. You do not need to believe the best explanation to ask a safe practical question.","The fact-and-prediction pair plus clarification is an ODA adaptation, not a validated stand-alone CBT treatment. A known mistake can call for repair. A documented unreasonable demand can call for negotiation or support. Abuse and coercion call for protection, not a more generous interpretation of the other person. NHS anxiety guidance distinguishes gradual work with safe feared situations from the need for care when anxiety disrupts life [C03]. This lesson is not exposure therapy."]},{heading:"Different emotion is not different external evidence",paragraphs:["Webb and colleagues summarized 306 experimental comparisons [C09]. Perspective change and stimulus reappraisal had average effects on emotion in predominantly short laboratory tasks. Thought suppression’s pooled estimate was −0.12. That does not establish that every attempt to put a thought aside fails, or that expression suppression is the same thing. Nor does feeling less anxious establish that a new interpretation is true.","The opened abstract did not provide confidence intervals or verify a total participant count. Lasting effects of this brief ODA worksheet remain untested. The practical purpose is to stop using a prediction as if it were already an observation, then choose a safe act relevant to your continuing experiment. Keep the response and its actual result together in the record. A question without a reply leaves uncertainty, which is still valid information for the next attempt or a support decision."]}],sourceIds:["C03","C09","C10"]}},{schemaVersion:2,revision:2,id:"calm-5",courseId:"calm",locale:"en",title:"Stop carrying the week alone",goal:"Review the same experiment and repeat one useful response or make a specific needed support request.",minutes:{learning:6,suggestedAction:2,review:2},direction:{hook:"Still carrying the week alone? Use any records to choose one next response or ask for actual support.",reason:{id:"calm-5-reason",label:"What do you need help carrying instead of another technique?",kind:"text",maxLength:160,optional:!0,suggestions:["Arranging a care appointment","Handling appointments while sleep is disrupted","A workload I cannot carry alone"]},why:{id:"calm-5-why",label:"What would this move protect in your week?",kind:"text",maxLength:300,optional:!0}},idea:{paragraphs:["Professional help fits when coping is difficult or self-help is not working. You do not need a crisis, a perfect explanation, or a finished course. A support list is preparation; asking for specific help is the contact step.","Review any records from your cue-and-response experiment. Keep a feasible response or ask for help. If you have not tried, choose a first safe opportunity. “Could you help me arrange an appointment?” is concrete. No reply is still a contact attempt. Recurrent panic or daily impairment warrants assessment; support need not wait for practice."],example:{title:"Priya asks for help with the load",text:"Priya’s records show three clear priority requests, but she is still struggling to sleep and manage care appointments. She asks her sister, “Could you help me call the clinic today?” and makes an assessment request. Her sister can only help tomorrow. Priya records both contacts and keeps one workload question for next week, without adding another exercise."}},move:{invitation:"Keep your plan; edit the clinic-contact example.",instructions:["Review records; repeat what fit or request needed support.","Before saving, name the actual recipient, ask, available cue, and fallback contact."],prompts:[],suggestions:{action:["Call the public clinic’s appointment desk","Ask sister to help arrange an appointment","Ask a friend for ten minutes together"],minimum:["Request assessment for sleep and appointment difficulties","Send sister an appointment support request","Send one request for a ten-minute conversation"],cue:["After lunch, during the desk’s opening hours","After lunch today","When the public clinic’s desk opens"],place:["At home with my phone","Kitchen table, using accessible messaging","A private room with accessible contact tools"],minutes:[2,5,10],fallback:["Use the clinic’s accessible appointment request form","Ask sister to help contact the clinic","Call the public clinic’s next opening"],observation:["Contact attempted; response and next access step","Whether practical support happened","Next access step, including no reply"],when:[{label:"Today",daysFromToday:0},{label:"Tomorrow",daysFromToday:1}]},suggested:{action:"Call the public clinic’s appointment desk",minimum:"Request assessment for sleep and appointment difficulties",minutes:2,cueHint:"After lunch, during the desk’s opening hours",placeHint:"At home with my phone",obstacleHint:"The clinic’s appointment desk does not answer",fallbackHint:"Use the clinic’s accessible appointment request form",observationHint:"Contact attempted; response and next access step",kind:"support"},stopRule:"Self-harm thoughts need prompt professional or crisis help. If you cannot stay safe or face immediate danger, use local emergency help.",accessibleAlternative:"Choose Adapt this, then Support for a contact plan. Use public-service, assisted, text, or phone contact. Name the recipient, ask, opening, and available fallback before saving."},result:{question:"Did you make the request or response you chose?",observationPrompt:"What did you ask or do, and what support actually followed?"},judgment:{primary:{id:"calm-5-decision",scenario:"Priya’s anxiety repeatedly disrupts sleep and appointments. She can stay safe. Her public clinic’s appointment desk is open now and accessible; a support line offers referrals. Her sister can help tomorrow.",question:"Which next plan fits the record?",options:[{id:"calm-5-support-and-care",text:"Contact the clinic today with a brief impact summary, then ask for any practical help needed.",feedback:"This uses an accessible assessment route without waiting for crisis or perfect certainty. Her sister can help later. If immediate safety changes, the urgent route becomes necessary."},{id:"calm-5-friends-first",text:"Ask her sister to help book an appointment tomorrow, while noting how the difficulties affect daily life.",feedback:"Assisted booking can make access easier. Here Priya can reach her known clinic herself today; waiting for tomorrow’s help delays available assessment. Her sister can still assist later."},{id:"calm-5-complete-explanation",text:"Call the public support line today to locate a clinician, then decide which appointment desk to contact.",feedback:"Referral advice helps when an accessible clinician is missing. Her own clinic is reachable now, so searching adds a step without answering her immediate access need. Both routes remain legitimate options."}],bestOptionId:"calm-5-support-and-care"}},carry:{takeaway:"Keep what fit. Ask for the help the remaining problem actually needs.",followUp:{questionId:"calm-5-return",question:"What happened with your request or response, and which reply or access step is still pending?",observationPrompt:"What happened when your chosen cue arrived?",adjustmentPrompt:"What should you repeat, change, or get help with?"}},goDeeper:{sections:[{heading:"A request has a recipient and a specific ask",paragraphs:["WHO and NHS guidance include trusted connections and practical coping options [C01, C02]. This course turns that advice into an optional specific request: a conversation, help with a task, or assistance contacting care. The script and fallback are ODA design, not a validated isolated intervention. Sending a request is an observable act. Receiving support, feeling better, and solving the stressor are separate outcomes.","Review any plans and attempts from your experiment instead of collecting another routine. Identify which response was usable, what interfered, and what remains beyond your capacity. An untried plan has no known outcome; choose a first safe opportunity or needed support. A person may be unavailable or decline; that is information for another contact or public service. You do not need a trusted personal network to ask for professional help. Keep sensitive details private if you prefer, while making a concrete next access step."]},{heading:"Assessment can begin before an emergency",paragraphs:["NHS stress guidance recommends professional help when coping is difficult or self-help is not working [C02]. Recurrent panic needs assessment because symptoms can have other causes and treatment choices may require clinical support [C04]. A new physical symptom should not automatically be labeled stress. This course does not prescribe medication changes, a fixed CBT session count, or a treatment bundle of sleep, movement, and breathing.","Self-harm thoughts without immediate danger still call for prompt professional or crisis support. If you cannot stay safe or face immediate danger, use local emergency help now and ask a trusted person to stay if safe. These are safety routes, not a diagnosis or validated triage algorithm. The final learning step can be keeping one workable response, resting, pausing the experiment, or making a care request. None requires completing a full set of techniques first."]}],sourceIds:["C01","C02","C04"]}},{schemaVersion:2,revision:1,id:"calm-6",courseId:"calm",locale:"en",title:"Tired but wired? Give your stress system a rhythm",goal:"Pick one daily rhythm habit and try it for a week with a cue, place, and two-minute minimum.",minutes:{learning:8,suggestedAction:2,review:2},direction:{hook:"Exhausted, yet unable to settle? Give one part of your day a predictable time this week.",reason:{id:"calm-6-reason",label:"Which part of your day needs a steadier rhythm?",kind:"text",maxLength:160,optional:!0,suggestions:["A bedtime that keeps drifting","Coffee that reaches into the afternoon","Training without enough recovery"]},why:{id:"calm-6-why",label:"What would a steadier day make easier for you?",kind:"text",maxLength:300,optional:!0}},idea:{paragraphs:["Feeling tired but wired has several causes; it does not reveal cortisol levels. Cortisol helps meet demands, usually rising toward morning and falling toward evening. The HPA axis links brain and adrenal glands to coordinate this response.","Choose one daily rhythm habit for seven days, with a cue, place, and two-minute start. Try a sleep window: keep bedtime and wake time within 30 minutes of your targets, leaving enough time to sleep. Two minutes starts the habit; it is not a studied treatment dose."],example:{title:"Deniz protects two times in her day",text:"During daytime shifts, Deniz chooses sleep timing for one week. When her evening show ends, she sets a lights-out alarm and charges her phone in the kitchen. She aims for 10:30 p.m. and 6:30 a.m., within half an hour. She records actual times and afternoon energy, without inferring a cortisol change."}},move:{invitation:"Choose one habit for seven days. Edit the sleep example to fit your week.",instructions:["Name one cue, place, and two-minute starting step.","Repeat for a week; record timing and one ordinary observation."],prompts:[],suggestions:{action:["Keep bedtime and wake time within 30 minutes","Walk outside after lunch","Finish coffee before noon"],minimum:["Set one alarm for lights-out","Step outside for two minutes","Set a coffee cutoff reminder"],cue:["When the evening show ends","After putting away my lunch plate","After breakfast"],place:["Bedroom, phone charging outside","An accessible path near home","Kitchen table"],minutes:[2,5,10],fallback:["Phone goes face down on the kitchen counter","Use an accessible indoor route","Choose a caffeine-free drink later"],observation:["Lights-out time and afternoon energy","Time outside and energy afterward","Last caffeine time and bedtime"],when:[{label:"Today",daysFromToday:0},{label:"Tomorrow",daysFromToday:1}]},suggested:{action:"Keep bedtime and wake time within 30 minutes",minimum:"Set one alarm for lights-out",minutes:2,cueHint:"When the evening show ends",placeHint:"Bedroom, phone charging outside",obstacleHint:"A late message pulls me back to the screen",fallbackHint:"Phone goes face down on the kitchen counter",observationHint:"Lights-out time and afternoon energy",kind:"practice"},stopRule:"New or concerning physical symptoms need medical assessment, urgently when severe. Do not change medication. Stop breathing or movement if dizzy or unwell. Supplements are optional; get advice during pregnancy and do not use ashwagandha in pregnancy.",accessibleAlternative:"For shifts or care duties, protect an adequate sleep window. Suitable seated movement or a quiet pause can replace walking."},result:{question:"Did your cue arrive, and what did you actually do?",observationPrompt:"Record the time and how you felt; neither proves a cortisol change."},judgment:{primary:{id:"calm-6-decision",scenario:"A study followed about 61,000 adults. More regular sleep was linked with lower mortality, even after adjustment.",question:"What does the sleep regularity research show?",options:[{id:"calm-6-association",text:"Regularity was associated with lower mortality; the study cannot prove it caused the difference.",feedback:"This was an observational study. Other differences may still matter; regular timing is a reasonable habit to try, not a causal guarantee."},{id:"calm-6-cause",text:"Keeping the same bedtime for a week proves that cortisol has returned to normal.",feedback:"The study did not test a one-week habit or establish a personal cortisol change. Your timing notes cannot measure hormone levels."},{id:"calm-6-cortisol",text:"Regularity caused lower mortality, so sleep duration no longer matters.",feedback:"Prediction is not causation. Adequate sleep time still matters alongside regularity; this study does not make sleep duration irrelevant to health."}],bestOptionId:"calm-6-association"}},carry:{takeaway:"Give one habit a reliable time before adding the next.",followUp:{questionId:"calm-6-return",question:"After a week, which timing held and what got in the way?",observationPrompt:"Compare actual times and your afternoon energy without diagnosing a hormone change.",adjustmentPrompt:"Keep the habit, reduce its starting step, or choose a more workable cue."}},goDeeper:{sections:[{heading:"Six choices, one habit this week",paragraphs:["Rafael Nasri's 2026 X article inspired the six habit choices below. This is ODA's adaptation, not a tested programme. Pick one for seven days and leave the others optional. For the sleep plan, choose bedtime and wake time within 30 minutes of your targets, with 7 to 9 hours available. After your evening cue, spend two minutes setting the alarm and moving your phone outside the bedroom. These timings are practical choices, not treatment thresholds.","Regular sleep was associated with lower mortality in 60,977 UK Biobank adults [calm-sleep-regularity]. Observation cannot show that changing bedtime caused the difference. Protect adequate sleep when shifts or care duties change your schedule. Record actual times and one ordinary observation each day. If the cue fails, change it or shrink the starting step before adding another habit."],visual:{kind:"steps",title:"Six choices for a steadier day",steps:[{label:"Sleep timing",text:"Bedtime and wake time within 30 minutes; protect enough sleep."},{label:"Walking",text:"After lunch, try an accessible route; start with two minutes."},{label:"Food",text:"Add greens, beans, nuts, or whole grains to a meal."},{label:"Coffee timing",text:"Try a noon cutoff; adapt to bedtime and sensitivity."},{label:"Activity and rest",text:"Choose suitable activity and leave room for recovery."},{label:"A buffer for hard days",text:"Use a comfortable pause or time with someone you trust."}],note:"Choose one this week, with a cue, place, and two-minute start."}},{heading:"What the research can tell you",paragraphs:["A review of 24 studies found that caffeine reduced sleep time by 45 minutes and delayed sleep onset by 9 minutes on average [calm-caffeine-meta]. Dose and sensitivity differ. Finishing coffee before noon is an editable starting choice, not a guarantee; count other caffeine too. The table separates study findings from what your own week can tell you.","Another review linked poorer sleep quality with stronger cortisol responses to laboratory stress [calm-hpa-sleep]. It did not establish that everyday tiredness diagnoses a hormone problem. These studies did not test this whole habit package, a two-minute dose, or your personal response. A week of timing and energy notes helps you judge whether the routine fits, not whether cortisol changed. Keep, adapt, or pause the habit; seek care when symptoms need assessment."],visual:{kind:"table",title:"A finding and its boundary",columns:["Topic","Study finding","Limit"],rows:[["Sleep regularity","60,977 adults; regular sleep was linked with lower mortality.","Observational association, not proof that a bedtime change prevents death."],["Caffeine","24 studies; less sleep and longer to fall asleep on average.","Dose and sensitivity vary; no universal safe cutoff."],["Sleep and cortisol","Poorer sleep quality was linked with stronger laboratory stress responses.","Not a diagnosis, daily hormone curve, or test of this plan."]],note:"Studies measured different outcomes; your timing notes do not measure hormones."}}],sourceIds:["calm-sleep-regularity","calm-caffeine-meta","calm-hpa-sleep"]}}],i=[{practice:["Notice a familiar body signal or outward situation cue; a physical symptom does not establish its cause.","Give the feeling a one-word name; it does not have to be the perfect word.","Describe the situation behind it in one sentence: “In this situation, I feel …”."],reflection:"Which sign most often tells you that you are stressed?",question:"Which view of stress fits this lesson?",options:["If I feel stressed, something must be badly wrong, and I need to get rid of it right away.","Stress is an ordinary response to difficult situations; noticing and naming it can be a first step.","Naming my feeling will only make it bigger, so I should ignore it."],correct:1,feedback:"Stress can accompany difficult situations. Noticing and naming may help you choose a useful response, but do not guarantee relief. New symptoms or difficulty coping can require assessment or support."},{practice:["If suitable, sit safely and try the studied five-minute routine voluntarily; ordinary breathing and an outward anchor are alternatives.","For cyclic sighing, inhale slowly, add a shorter inhale without forcing fullness, then exhale slowly. Stop if dizzy or unwell.","Note comfort, actual practice, or a stop separately from mood. New or concerning symptoms need assessment; do not drive while symptomatic."],reflection:"What did you notice in your body when you lengthened your exhale?",question:"What is the emphasis in cyclic sighing?",options:["Holding your breath for as long as possible.","Taking fast, deep breaths one after another.","Keeping the exhale slow and long."],correct:2,feedback:"Cyclic sighing uses two inhales followed by a slow exhale. Five daily minutes for 28 days was studied, not a quick reset. Stop if dizzy or unwell and return to ordinary breathing."},{practice:["Choose suitable movement today, including an accessible seated version, or keep a nonmovement pause if movement is unsuitable.","Link it to a moment that already happens in your day, such as a short walk after lunch.","If useful, record tension before and after moving; unchanged feelings are valid, and a change does not prove causation."],reflection:"Which kind of movement feels like a break to you, rather than a punishment?",question:"How should the 2023 umbrella review by Singh and colleagues be read?",options:["Movement is linked with fewer symptoms, but most of the reviews were of low quality.","Everyone who exercises will definitely lose their anxiety.","If I exercise, I no longer need medication or therapy."],correct:0,feedback:"Activity programs reduced symptoms on average, but most included reviews were critically low quality. Their median results do not predict one short break or replace needed care."},{practice:["Write down, in one sentence, a situation that has been stressing you lately.","Consider plausible alternatives without inventing motives; an uncertain explanation may be more accurate than a positive one.","Imagine how the situation would look to a neutral observer, then say to yourself the sentence you would say to a friend going through the same thing."],reflection:"When you read the situation from another angle, what changed and what stayed the same?",question:"In the 2012 meta-analysis by Webb and colleagues, which stood out as a more effective strategy?",options:["Trying not to think about the event at all.","Trying to see the situation through the eyes of an outside observer.","Getting stuck on the negative feeling and dwelling on it."],correct:1,feedback:"Perspective change and reappraisal affected short laboratory emotions on average. The pooled thought-suppression estimate was negative, not proof that suppression always fails. Use evidence and a safe next action."},{practice:["Name available trusted or public-service contacts; a personal support network is not required to request care.","Choose a moment this week to tell someone how you are doing, or to ask for a small bit of support.","Using the help table, write down in your own notes whom you will turn to in which situation."],reflection:"On a hard day, who is the first person you would want to reach?",question:"If you cannot stay safe or are in immediate danger, what should you do?",options:["Finish the rest of the course exercises and wait.","Handle it on my own so I don’t burden anyone.","Call my local emergency number and, if possible, ask someone to be with me."],correct:2,feedback:"Use local emergency help if you cannot stay safe or face immediate danger. Self-harm thoughts without immediate danger still require prompt professional or crisis support; do not wait to finish the course."},{practice:["Choose one of the six habits for the next seven days.","Write its cue, place, and two-minute minimum; set the reminder now.","Record when it happened and one ordinary observation each day."],reflection:"Which small part of your day could become more predictable?",question:"What does the sleep regularity research show?",options:["Regularity was associated with lower mortality; the study cannot prove it caused the difference.","Keeping the same bedtime for a week proves that cortisol has returned to normal.","Regularity caused lower mortality, so sleep duration no longer matters."],correct:0,feedback:"This was an observational study. Other differences may still matter; regular timing is a reasonable habit to try, not a causal guarantee."}],n=[{id:"1758876019128-e76eebf402bd",alt:"Tired person resting their head on one hand at a table"},{id:"1717691526463-975c4119505c",alt:"Woman standing with her eyes closed in front of a tree"},{id:"1758272421829-7028296dee8f",alt:"Woman stretching on the floor of a living room"},{id:"1658279366796-e0c28623cd27",alt:"Woman sitting on a windowsill looking outside"},{id:"1747764925585-6c2df83b3fd2",alt:"Two women talking over coffee"},{id:"1759193513693-97638b7f1160",alt:"Two people walking a dog on a park path"}],s={id:"calm",title:"Stress is choosing your replies? Take the next move back",subtitle:"Six lessons to catch one stress cue, try a safe response, and give one daily habit a steadier rhythm.",description:"Build one cue-and-response experiment around your real week. Use a comfortable pause, suitable movement, fact checking, or support only when it helps your chosen act.",scope:"Everyday stress-management education for adults, not diagnosis or treatment. Stop breathing or movement exercises if dizzy, uncomfortable, or unwell; sit safely and return to ordinary breathing. New or concerning physical symptoms need medical assessment, urgently when severe. Do not assume panic explains them. Seek professional care for recurrent panic, impaired daily life, difficulty coping, or unsuccessful self-help. Do not change treatment or medication independently. Self-harm thoughts need prompt professional or crisis support; inability to stay safe or immediate danger needs local emergency help. Abuse calls for safety and specialist support, not tolerance practice.",outcome:"One safe response you can use at your stress cue, honest attempt records, and a workable next-week or support step.",photo:{id:"1557316655-8715fdecd2d1",alt:"Still lake at the foot of a mountain"},lessons:a.map((e,t)=>({id:e.id,schemaVersion:2,v2:e,title:e.title,minutes:e.minutes.learning,goal:e.goal,reading:e.idea.paragraphs,...i[t],takeaway:e.carry.takeaway,sources:t===0?[...e.goDeeper.sourceIds,"C01"]:e.goDeeper.sourceIds,deeper:e.goDeeper.sections,example:{title:`Invented example: ${e.idea.example.title}`,text:e.idea.example.text},photo:n[t]}))};export{s as COURSE,a as LESSONS_V2,o as SOURCES};
